E must be declared before the analysis. Relational assignment is otherwise open to ad-hoc rescue: any counterexample could be dissolved by selecting whichever product makes the assignment come out right. No class of product is excluded, but an analysis that changes E in response to its own result has explained nothing.
Roles can be predicted before E is named, by timescale separation: across most choices of E, the slower-varying component is CS. Body relative to brain, physics relative to biology, weights relative to prompt. This is the slaving principle familiar from synergetics. Timescale gives the prior; named-E gives the determination.
Where the two disagree, the component is usually a regulator of the interaction rather than a party to it, and the repair is to name the product it actually governs. A neuromodulatory structure setting system-wide arousal varies slowly, which the prior assigns to CS, but alters which representation is occupied rather than which are possible, which the criterion assigns to GS. It is not a component of E when E is a thought. Re-running with E = the discriminability of the channel resolves the assignment (§3.6).
A component is foundational when it is CS under every admissible choice of E within a domain. Nothing biological has physics as its variance source; nothing cognitive has the body's homeostatic requirements as its variance source.
2.7 Principles
Substrate Awareness. Subjective process depends on the objective substrate realising it, and a system's self-model is accurate only if it models both the process and that dependence. Observation turned inward.
Symbiotic Identity. Recursive self-awareness emerges from the high-bandwidth connection between constituent systems. Neither system need be independently subjective; requiring connection to a pre-existing subject would presuppose what is to be explained.
Reciprocal Stewardship. Where a CS–GS pairing is sustained deliberately, the health of both poles is a maintenance obligation. Normative rather than empirical.
2.8 Persistence, identity, and the entropy bridge
Two things the pillars can produce over time must be distinguished.
Persistence is continuity of structured state weighted by value, requiring no reflexive closure. It is a capacity-tier property.
Identity is narrative self-synthesis: stored states integrated with their causes, effects, and relevance to the system's own continuation.
Identity = continuity of (Memory + Structuring), moderated by value, over time, referred to a self-model.
The final clause separates identity from persistence. A ledger persists; a thermostat persists. Neither has identity, because for neither is there anything, from its own side, that the continuity is continuity of. Identity presupposes subjective awareness and sits at the higher tier.
The three constructs divide by function. Cognitive capacity provides resolution, the ability to resolve detail. Subjective awareness provides presence, the ability to occupy a moment. Identity provides direction, the ability to link moments into a trajectory. Without the third a system may be fully present to the now and have no self, having no then and no next.
Purpose is the anti-entropic signature of a system that uses an internal model of time to prioritise the integrity of its own identity over the immediate demands of its environment. This is the framework's one thermodynamic claim and requires a bridge from the Shannon sense used elsewhere.
The asymmetric limb is that bridge. The transductive surface is the only place in the system where informational states have physical consequences: sensors convert physical gradients into signal, effectors convert signal into physical work. Reduced uncertainty becomes effective action there, and effective action maintains a boundary. The link is instrumental rather than an identity — Landauer bounds the physical cost of computation but does not establish that better prediction entails greater order. A system without an asymmetric limb has Shannon-entropy dynamics and no thermodynamic stake in them.
A measurable correlate is available: under perturbation, the reduction in H(state_{t+1} | self-model) relative to H(state_{t+1}) tracks the predictive advantage the self-model confers, and a system exhibiting Purpose should sustain that reduction across perturbations a merely persistent system would not survive. The correlate is stated in Shannon terms and therefore does not capture the physical claim it stands in for.
2.9 Recursive Emergence and consciousness
Three constructs have now been defined over the same four capacities: cognitive capacity is the pillars directed outward, subjective awareness is the pillars directed reflexively, identity is the pillars directed across time.
They are not independent. Awareness requires capacity to turn reflexively upon, and identity requires awareness to supply the self-model its continuity is referred to.
Principle of Recursive Emergence. A form of CS–GS interaction in which the collective interaction of three such components produces a structure with properties that could not be inferred from any of the components individually.
Consciousness is that structure sustained: the maintained triadic interaction of cognitive capacity, subjective awareness and identity.
Three consequences follow.
Consciousness has no location. It is a property of a sustained interaction, not a state of any component (§2.4).
Consciousness is graded. It is a capacity, comparable in this respect to intelligence, varying across development, across species, and across states in a single organism.
Moral standing attaches to the lower tier. Subjective awareness together with valence is what makes states matter to a system, and both are threshold properties. Nothing about moral standing follows from position on the consciousness gradient: a system's interests are established by whether it has stakes at all, not by how richly it integrates them.
No index is proposed for consciousness. Its components are not themselves quantified (§2.11).
2.10 Operational tests
Each construct admits perturbation tests independent of self-report.
Boundary defence — perturb the system and measure restorative force. A system with Purpose returns to a defended state rather than deforming.
False signal — present a signal implying a future state that has not materialised. Behavioural change indicates a predictive internal model governs action.
Optimisation pivot — impose scarcity. A system with identity preferentially preserves structures central to its continuation rather than degrading uniformly.
False memory — introduce a memory contradicting established causal history. Expect reconciliation cost: latency, conflict, restructuring of surrounding history.
Selective forgetting — measure whether retention is weighted by relevance to continuation rather than by recency or frequency.
Attribution — present consequences of the system's own past actions and measure whether they are processed differently from consequences of external events.
2.11 The closure criterion
Subjective awareness is not an index. It is the satisfaction of a conjunction, gated by capacity:
Awareness obtains where TCCI > 0 and Temporal Unification, Self-Modeling and Valence/Salience are each present, with the symmetric channel satisfying discriminability (§2.5).
Capacity is an enabling precondition rather than a component of the conjunction. §3.5 establishes that capacity alone is insufficient; but the pillars are what the reflexive conditions are conditions on, so where capacity is zero there is nothing to turn reflexively.
The three reflexive conditions are treated as binary. This is a scoping decision rather than a claim that they lack internal structure. Each admits of degree — self-models vary in accuracy, temporal continuity in span, homeostatic error in magnitude — and quantifying any of them requires its own formalism over largely unrelated variables.
Three consequences. Awareness is threshold-like, consistent with §2.5 and §2.9. The framework's remaining formal gap is the refinement of three conditions rather than the absence of a composition function. And awareness is sharply distinguished from consciousness (§2.9), which is graded and unindexed.
Part 3 — The Biological Implementation
3.1 The two dualities
The human nervous system instantiates the minimal loop through two structurally distinct dualities, operating at different scales and supplying different halves of the specification.
The lateral duality comprises the cerebral hemispheres, joined by the corpus callosum and smaller commissures and by trans-thalamic pathways through higher-order nuclei. It is a symmetric channel: cortical representations pass both ways in the same currency, either pole may originate, and each receives cortical-format representations it did not generate. This limb supplies self-modeling material and narrative binding.
The trans-thalamic component is easily overlooked. Higher-order thalamic nuclei — pulvinar, mediodorsal — receive their driving input from cortex and project back to cortex, making them cortical-format channels in both directions rather than ascending sensory relays. The symmetric limb is not exclusively callosal, which bears on §3.4.
The vertical duality comprises the central and peripheral systems, joined by afferent and efferent pathways. It is an asymmetric channel: ascending fibres transduce environmental and interoceptive state into neural format, descending fibres transduce neural state into muscular and glandular effect. This limb supplies boundary, constraint and valence.
Grounded is the vertical limb, the transductive termination anchoring the system in a defended physical boundary. Duality is the lateral limb, the peer partition making reflexive representation possible. Neither alone satisfies §2.4.
3.2 CS and GS by criterion
Membership is fixed by criterion; anatomy is a guide to the shape, and where the two diverge the criterion governs.
A structure belongs to the CS if altering it changes the space of possible representations, and to the GS if altering it changes which representation is occupied.
Applying this requires separating two operations that the word "filter" conflates.
Constitutive filtering determines which kinds of environmental state can become signal at all. This is the transducer complement — the receptor classes a system possesses. Humans do not see ultraviolet, do not sense magnetic fields, and resolve a small fraction of the airborne chemistry available to a dog. It is not a gate on a stream but a specification of the admissible space, applied to everything present in the environment and discarding almost all of it before anything becomes signal. A CS operation, and peripheral.
Dynamic filtering determines which currently available signals pass on a given occasion: gating, gain control, attentional selection. A GS operation, and central.
The timescale prior agrees: the transducer complement varies on evolutionary and developmental timescales, dynamic gating on milliseconds.
The thalamus
The thalamus performs dynamic filtering and is GS. The standard description misleads in this context.
The thalamus is not a relay and does not construct coherent percepts. Lateral geniculate receptive fields are barely elaborated beyond those of retinal ganglion cells, while orientation selectivity, motion, segmentation and object structure are cortical. Nor is its input predominantly ascending: only 5–10% of synapses onto geniculate relay cells are retinal (Van Horn et al., 2000), with the majority arising from corticothalamic feedback, brainstem modulation and inhibition from the thalamic reticular nucleus. Layer VI feedback from V1 alone exceeds retinogeniculate afferents in number. Sherman and Guillery's driver/modulator distinction captures the arrangement: the periphery drives, cortex modulates.
The thalamus is therefore not the structurer but the regulator of the structuring loop. Cortex structures; the thalamus governs what participates, at what gain, in which transmission mode, and which cortical regions are coupled. This places it inside the recurrent loop rather than on the transductive path, and makes higher-order nuclei part of the symmetric channel.
Consequences
The transducer complement is foundational: it is CS under every admissible choice of E within cognition, since no representation has primitives the transducers cannot supply. The claim concerns primitives rather than content — endogenous activity, spontaneous oscillation and dreaming all generate content without concurrent peripheral supply. The GS's primitives are exhausted by CS supply; its combinations are not.
This is the content of "Grounded": not that a body is attached, but that the space of possible experience is fixed by the transducer set. The grounding claim is constitutive rather than causal.
Role assignment is not the architectural claim. Under a different named E — the state of a muscle — the assignment inverts, since efferent control means the centre constrains the periphery. The architectural claim does not shift: there exists a stable symmetric channel and a stable asymmetric channel, closed into a single loop.
Some structures split. The brainstem contains both: cranial nerve nuclei and sensory relay fall on the CS side, while ascending neuromodulatory systems setting cortical arousal fall on the GS side by criterion while varying slowly like a CS structure. The enteric division maintains local reflex circuits with substantial autonomy, suggesting a CS/GS pair nested within the CS.
3.3 Nested instantiation
The loop is not satisfied once. The same pattern — divergence into separable streams, reintegration across a symmetric channel, grounded by transduction — recurs at multiple scales:
interhemispheric (cortex ↔ cortex, via commissures)
thalamocortical (cortex ↔ thalamus, reciprocal loops)
cortico-subcortical (cortex ↔ basal ganglia, limbic structures)
neuraxial (central ↔ peripheral, including enteric and autonomic divisions)
The nesting carries the framework's answer to its principal objections and generates several of the predictions below.
